{"markdown_body": "1. The transaction fails due to an immediate counterparty default where the approved obligor enters insolvency or breaches foundational covenants prior to settlement and before any grace-period negotiation can occur. The early signal that would show it first is an abrupt widening of the counterparty's credit default swap spreads or sudden rating agency downgrades immediately preceding the funding window.

2. The transaction fails due to regulatory stops that abruptly freeze or prohibit the settlement of the approved obligation due to compliance, sanctions, or legal intervention. The early signal that would show it first is an unexpected administrative compliance flag or automated AML/sanctions screening hold triggered during pre-settlement clearing.

3. The transaction fails due to sudden liquidity shocks where a systemic or localized cash-flow freeze leaves the approved counterparty unable to fund its cleared commitments on presentation. The early signal that would show it first is an intraday spike in overnight lending/repo rates or an acute, unprecedented drawdown on the obligor's backup liquidity facilities.", "node_id": "plan"}
